an they  can you know prove to be an asset to  your business and that you both like  working with each other because it is  like getting married getting a partner  with your business so give them  milestones so they you know accomplish  something to get two percent and then  five percent and ten percent and until  whatever goal eventually you want to get  to I make sure you have really good  shareholds agreements of that there's a  shotgun agreement in there that you can  you know spy each other out if the  relationship doesn't go very well but  hopefully you've tested the beginning  and you don't get to the point where  you're you know buying each other out so  that's the second option the third  option is you can go up raise capital so  you could say you know we need another  you know X thousands of dollars people  to finish up this project go find an  angel investor give up a percentage of  your business the challenge here is if  you're pre-sales if you haven't finished  your actual product and you're still in  kind of the research and development  mode  really hard to raise money and the money  that you raised you'll get really killed  on the valuation so if you had some  sales you had some momentum then you get  a much better price you don't have to  give as much equity up in return for the  the money that you're giving away if you  want to try to mitigate that risk a  little bit what you can do is put it in  stages so you can say i need $100,000  say for the business I need you know  twenty five thousand dollars up front  and it's going to be a certain valuation  and then we're going to go out and try  to hit these milestones so we're going  to you know make X number of sales when  we do that we need another twenty-five  thousand dollars but at a better  valuation and then you go and hit more  milestones and you get the last fifty  thousand dollars a much better valuation  so you negotiate that up front so that  as you grow with your business you're  getting more money coming in from the  investor but a better evaluation then if  you need everything up front so I don't  know how much you need maybe one hundred  thousand dollars maybe it's ten thousand  dollars maybe it's half a million  dollars whatever it is you can phase it  out with an investor so that you're not  getting all the money up front but you  know you're also getting much better  valuation so hopefully that helps I  think your best bet is still to try to  go the outsourcing route at the start  and try to piece it off in the little  projects and find somebody who you can  work with longer-term solution try to  find somebody local who might be able to  take an equity position in the company  has a vested interest wants to make it  work and you have somebody sitting next  to you who knows motivated to want to  keep growing this product and as you get  more ideas you have somebody who you can  turn to and they you know really want to  see it succeed and last options go out  and try to find an investor just try not  to get to kill an evaluation by spacing  up the payment periods hopefully that  helps for those watching if you liked  the video please give it a thumbs up I  love seeing those come in and if you  have any thoughts please feel free to  leave a comment underneath any other  advice that you might have or any of  your own experiences or have other  questions leave a comment under the  video and i will see you on the next  episode  you
 

